
    Validator 1.2 Copyright (c) 1999 CDI, cdi@thewebmasters.net

    + Modified since 1.1
    * New in 1.2

*   get_perms          Returns a four element array showing the permissions
                       for a file.  This obviously only applies to Unix
                       hosts. The array has the following structure:

                       octal permission, string user, sting group, string other

                       The keys for the array are "octal", "user", "group",
                       "other", so to retreive the octal representation of a
                       file's permissions, use the following:

                       $perms = $check->get_perms($fileName);
                       echo "Octal: $perms["octal"] \n";

                       The "user","group","other" keys have values matching
                       that returned from an 'ls -l' command, so if the
                       file's octal value were 744, user,group, and other
                       would be as follows:

                       "user"  == "rwx"
                       "group" == "r--"
                       "other" == "r--"

                       If the file happens to be a directory, the "user"
                       value will be "dxxx" (so on the above example, it
                       would be "drwx").  So all this means that if you want
                       to know if a file is readable by group, you can do it
                       in 2 lines of code:

                      $perms = $check->get_perms($fileName);
                      if(ereg("r",$perms["group"]))
                      {... file is readable by group...}

                      If for any reason the method can't figure out what the
                      permissions are the method sets that section to "unk"


+    CLEAR             Changed the clear var so that it only calls
                       clear_error() in functions that can generate a new
                       ERROR string. If the function can't generate a new
                       ERROR msg, it doesn't bother clearing old ones.

*    strip_html        removes all html elements, tags, attributes, etc
                       ALL html gets stripped leaving only plain text.
                       Not very efficient, fairly slow, but accurate.

+    is_country        Found 14 country codes I was missing and one duplicated
                       entry. Now verifies 243 country codes.

+    browser_gen       Added 24 spiders to the list of known agents
+    is_host           Modified to accept CNAME only records as valid


